From 90f57806e1cd501d065c47d1bb6a8da370e8912b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitry B <dimitryB@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 11:58:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] eip155: a lagging RPC cannot hide a settlement's receipt

A read goes to every RPC and the first answer wins, and null from a node
that has not seen the block is an answer. A fast RPC that lags therefore
hid a receipt the other RPC had, for as long as it lagged. The transfer
was mined and payTo paid, but the settle and every retry answered
settlement_pending. A stock seller retries once and then refuses, so the
buyer was charged and not served.

That took two routes. A retry read the receipt through the shared client.
The first settle waited in alloy's get_receipt, which ends the wait with
an error if one read is null once its block watcher has seen the
transaction, so a brief lag was enough, in under a second. The watcher
reads through the shared client too, so a node frozen at an old block
held the whole 60 s receipt wait.

find_receipt asks each RPC on its own and takes the first real receipt.
Only a receipt ends the search early, and an RPC that stalls holds up no
other. Both routes use it, so the first settle polls every RPC in place
of alloy's watcher, every 7 s as alloy did, with a last look when the
wait ends. With no RPC holding the receipt the answer is
settlement_pending, as before.

The wait no longer reads block numbers, so a receipt is the one
confirmation a settlement waits for. MetaTransaction.confirmations was
always 1 and is gone.

The lookups now run through each RPC's rate limiter, shared with the
fallback client. Every settlement makes them now, so the 4 per second on
the public RPC has to cover them.

